uation: I hold a pair of 6s with a weak kicker on a board of 2c, 6h, 4s, 9c, Jh. This gives me the third pair on the board. The opponent has taken an aggressive line, betting on the Flop, Turn, and River. This "triple barrel" sequence usually indicates a strong value hand (Top Pair, Overpair, Set, or Straight). While the opponent could be bluffing with a missed draw (like 5-7 or a flush draw), the Jack on the river connects with many high-card hands that might have been semi-bluffing earlier. To call profitably, I would need to beat the opponent\'s hand about 31% of the time. Given the strength represented and the dangerous river card, it is highly probable my hand is beaten by Jx, 9x, or higher pairs.\n\nReason: My hand is a mere bluff-catcher (3rd pair) facing strong aggression on all three post-flop streets. The probability of the opponent having a better hand (J, 9, 77+, straight) is high, and the potential reward does not justify the risk of calling a large river bet. Folding minimizes the loss for this hand.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
